The '''Black Temple''' is the fortress-citadel of [[Illidan Stormrage]], Lord of Outland. It was once known as the '''Temple of Karabor''', and has changed hands many times over multiple generations. The entrance to the Black Temple does not appear to be its main gates, but rather an instance portal set in a breach in the southern battlement, below the [[ruins of Karabor]].{{coords|71.0|46.3|Shadowmoon Valley}} It no longer has a required attunement.

==Background==
[[File:Karabor air.jpg|thumb|The Temple of Karabor before its fall.]]
{{Stub-section}} <!-- Needs a lot of cleanup, and adding the various stories of the Temple being used as a demon hunter training ground, the Council of the Black Harvest, the Illidari attacking the Temple against the Ashtongue in Legion, the Illidan novel, etc. -->
Before the shattering of [[Draenor]], the Black Temple was once called the Temple of Karabor, a sacred site to the [[draenei]] which served as the refuge of the Prophet [[Velen]]. The temple was designed by [[Exarch Hataaru]]<ref>[[An Old Wound]]</ref> and was built atop the most important [[ley line]] nexus of the planet.<ref name="BTDP 17">''[[Beyond the Dark Portal]]'', chapter 17</ref>

===Rise of the Horde===
{{novel-section}}
As the [[Old Horde|Horde]] started its [[genocide of the draenei]], they eventually discovered that the [[Burning Legion]] was manipulating the [[orc]]s. The draenei leaders knew they could no longer defend their holdings and ordered their forces to withdraw to [[Shattrath City]] and Karabor.<ref>''[[World of Warcraft: Chronicle Volume 2]], pg. 84 - 85''</ref> On its side, the Horde decided to conquer the Temple in order to shatter the draenei as a people, destroy everything that meant anything to them.<ref>''[[Rise of the Horde]]'', chapter 18</ref> During the ensuing battle against [[Gul'dan]]'s assault, the draenei were helped by [[arakkoa]] such as [[Grizzik]],<ref>''[[Beyond the Dark Portal]]'', chapter 24.</ref> and against all odds the outnumbered defenders were able to push the relentless orcs back.

In response, Gul'dan and the [[Shadow Council]] decided to ignore the [[Shadowmoon clan]]'s warnings about the Dark Star, an ancient god of the Void that was actually the fallen [[naaru]] [[K'ara]]. They conducted a ritual to call on the Dark Star in their war against the draenei and channeled it at Karabor. A pillar of [[Void]] energy ripped through the heavens and slammed down on Karabor, driving many draenei defenders to madness while many others died instantly. Velen called on his powers to keep the Void at bay and rally the survivors to Karabor's harbor, but the Dark Star had forever darkened Karabor, and it became known as the Black Temple and was taken by the orcs.<ref>''[[World of Warcraft: Chronicle Volume 2]]'', pg. 87</ref> After their defeat, the captured draenei [[cleric]]s of Karabor were marched out, told that they were to be taken to [[Hellfire Citadel]] as prisoners of war. The warlock [[Teron Gorefiend|Teron'gor]] murdered each and every last one of them in cold blood outside of the walls.<ref>[[Divination: Gorefiend's Cloak]]</ref> While Hellfire Citadel was [[Warchief]] [[Blackhand]]'s capital, Karabor became the clandestine Shadow Council's seat of ruling.

It was at Karabor that [[Mannoroth]] was first summoned to Draenor, and it was in the Temple's central courtyard's pool that the [[Annihilan|pit lord]] shed its [[Felblood|blood]] for the [[Blood-curse|unholy communion]] that would later happen at the [[Throne of Kil'jaeden]] in [[Hellfire Peninsula|Tanaan Jungle]].<ref>''[[Rise of the Horde]]'', chapter 19</ref>

At some point, the orc [[J'argg Bloodfyre]] came here and obtained some of Mannoroth's blood.<ref>''[[Legends Volume 2]]'', ''[[Family Values]]''</ref>

===The Burning Crusade===
{{bc-section}}
[[File:Black temple air.jpg|thumb|The Black Temple from the air.]]
During the [[Invasion of Draenor]], it was used as a [[Fortress Shadowmoon|fortress]] by [[Ner'zhul]] and his Shadowmoon clan forces. The Warchief cast his [[Spell of Conjuration]] there, hoping to tap into [[Draenor]]'s [[ley line]]s as a source of energy.<ref name="BTDP 17" /> After the planet's destruction and its transformation into [[Outland]], the Black Temple was taken over by the [[Burning Legion]]. It was used as a prime staging ground for the Burning Legion's campaign on Draenor.<ref>[[Underdev/Black Temple]]</ref>

When [[Illidan the Betrayer]] fled to [[Outland]] after the [[Third War]], he ousted the Temple's ruler—the pit lord [[Magtheridon]]—and claimed his throne as the lord of the blasted world. Following his defeat at the hands of the [[death knight]] [[Arthas Menethil]] in [[Icecrown]], Illidan returned to the Black Temple. According to [[Malfurion]], Illidan was consumed by his defeat, replaying the events in his mind repeatedly as he envisioned himself as the victor instead of the soon-to-be [[Lich King]].<ref>[[Waking Legends#Notes]]</ref> After the [[Dark Portal]] reopened, Illidan tightened his grip on Outland from within the profaned sanctum, where he awaited any and all who would challenge his rule.<ref>[https://web.archive.org/web/20110507213738im_/http://us.battle.net/wow/en/zone/black-temple/ Black Temple]</ref> However, Illidan often lived in fear that he would be discovered by his enemies, such as Arthas or the lords of the Burning Legion.<ref>[[The Burning Crusade Townhall/Outland]]</ref>

The Black Temple was besieged once by the [[Sha'tar]] forces of [[Marcus Auralion|High General Auralion]], but these draenei were destroyed by [[Val'zareq the Conqueror]] of the [[Crimson Sigil]] and their leader captured.<ref>[[The Journal of Val'zareq: Portends of War]]</ref> Some times later, the Burning Legion unleashed the [[fel reaver]] [[Doomwalker]] on the Temple.

Later, another army combining the forces of the [[Scryers]] and [[Aldor]] attacked again, this time helped by the Illidari rebel [[Akama]] and [[Maiev Shadowsong]], former prisoner of the Betrayer.

===Mists of Pandaria===
{{mop-section}}
[[Kanrethad Ebonlocke]] and [[Jubeka Shadowbreaker]] infiltrated the Black Temple, evading the [[Ashtongue Deathsworn]] under Akama who had reclaimed the temple after Illidan's death. Entering the [[Shrine of Lost Souls]], Kanrethad recognized it as the power source that allowed Illidan to control demons without their being corrupted by the fel energies endemic to the Legion, and theorized that it had been originally intended as the power source to replace the [[Sunwell]] when [[Kael'thas Sunstrider]] and his [[blood elf|blood elves]] aligned with him. Since Illidan's death, it grew in strength and power, waiting to be harnessed. The two warlocks prepared a [[Empowered Soulcore|crystal]] to absorb the arcane magics within the Shrine, leaving it to do its work when they heard Ashtongue patrols hunting them.

Later, an [[adventurer]] who studied warlock magics soon came on their trail. Reclaiming the now-empowered crystal and taking it to the summit, the warlock witnessed the opening of a massive gateway, and from it stepped Kanrethad in a monstrous [[Metamorphosis (warlock ability)|demonic form]], utterly corrupted by absorbing too much fel energy. Though Kanrethad's powers were formidable, the warlock persevered long enough for Jubeka to arrive and fulfill Kanrethad's earlier request to banish him.<ref>[[Pursuing the Black Harvest]]</ref>

===Legion===
{{Legion-section}}
Following [[Infiltrating the Black Temple|the break-in]] by the [[Council of the Black Harvest]], [[Akama]] had his [[Sha'tar]]i [[priest]]s erect a holy barrier<ref>[[The Arcane Way]]</ref> to protect the temple from [[fel]] magic.<ref>[[Move Like No Other]]</ref> The [[Illidari]], seeking to secure Akama and his knowledge about the Legion, sent Illidari-loyal [[Broken]] to infiltrate it and gain any valuable information.<ref>[[Back in Black (quest)]]</ref> [[Matron Mother Malevolence]] bypassed the holy barrier using the [[:Grimoire of Arcane Ways]], and the Illidari invaded the temple.<ref>[[Confrontation at the Black Temple]]</ref>

